  "notes": "West leads the ♠4 which South is forced to win in dummy. South has 2 spade tricks, the ♥A, and the ♦A in top tricks. 4 additional tricks can be developed in clubs once the ♣A is dislodged. The diamond suit can be counted on for the ninth trick.\n\nSouth should not make the mistake of playing on clubs at trick 2. East might win the ♣A and play another spade through South's holding. If West has the ♠Q, the defensive spades will be established. If West also has the ♦K, that card will act as an entry to cash the spades.\n\nAdmittedly, South is unlucky to go down after playing clubs at trick 2. However, South can virtually guarantee his contract by taking the diamond finesse at trick 2. If East has the ♦K, the finesse will win. South can turn his attention to clubs.\n\nIf West has the ♦K, he can win the diamond finesse, but cannot attack South's spade holding from his side of the table. Again, South will be able to knock out the ♣A and take 9 tricks.",
